WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:06.789
Space hotels with pools, gyms, bars, and gourmet
food have been one of the most common fantasies

00:00:06.789 --> 00:00:12.480
shown in sci fi space movies. Given that only
553 people have ever reached low earth orbit

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.990
and only 24 people have gone past low earth
orbit, it seems like space hotels won't be

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:22.050
a thing for decades if not centuries. So,
you might be surprised to hear that people

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:28.300
may be staying in space hotels by the end
of this year. If you think about it, we technically

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.450
already have a space hotel which is the ISS.
Dozens of astronauts visit and stay on the

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:39.980
ISS for extended periods of every single year.
According to google though, a hotel is an

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:44.760
establishment providing accommodations, meals,
and other services for travelers and tourists.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.610
Since ISS astronauts are employees and crew
members as opposed to travelers or tourists,

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:54.460
we can’t technically say that the ISS is
a space hotel right now. It should be noted

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.330
that though 7 private citizens have traveled
to the ISS throughout history meaning that

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:03.570
the ISS has been a space hotel in the past.
Surprisingly, Nasa actually wants to make

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:07.470
this a regular occurrence as they announced
that they were opening up the ISS for private

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:12.780
visitors in June of 2019. Nasa has been conducting
experiments and research on the ISS for two

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.890
decades at this point. They claim that there’s
still plenty of research to be done in low

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:21.270
earth orbit, but at the same time, they’re
looking to move onto bigger things like returning

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:26.290
to the moon and building a lunar base. So,
opening up the ISS for commercial tourism

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:31.470
is unfortunately the early stages of NASA
phasing out the ISS. Nonetheless, it does

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.820
make for an extremely cool tourist destination
in the meantime. Nasa revealed that they would

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:40.620
be allowing up to 4 private astronauts inside
the station at a time, and they would be charging

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:45.570
just $35,000 per day per visiting spacecraft.
This means that you can send as many people

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:50.510
as you can fit into a single rocket for the
same daily price of $35,000. If you were to

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:55.659
send a starship filled with 100 passengers,
each passenger would only be paying $350 per

00:01:55.659 --> 00:01:59.883
day. Given that only 4 private astronauts
are allowed inside the station itself at any

00:01:59.883 --> 00:02:04.409
given time though, it would be best to travel
with a group of less than 10 people. Each

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:08.450
visiting group will be allowed to stay at
the ISS for up to 30 days and Nasa will accept

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:12.690
two groups of visitors every single year.
Nasa was originally aiming to have private

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:18.200
astronauts on board by the end of 2020, but
clearly that didn’t happen. The problem

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.980
is that though the price to rent out the ISS
is actually extremely cheap, it's extremely

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:27.310
difficult to source a rocket and fund a rocket
launch. A startup called Axiom space is working

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:32.069
on accomplishing this though. In March of
2020, Axiom was actually able to book a crew

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:37.440
dragon launch with SpaceX that was supposed
to happen at the end of 2021. Since then,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:43.080
it has been pushed back to the start of 2022,
but there’s no signs of the trip being cancelled

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.890
or anything like that. The plan is to send
three tourists on a 10 day trip to the ISS.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:53.410
2 days will be spent in transit, but the tourists
will have a full 8 days onboard the ISS. Though

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:58.650
the price to rent out the ISS for 8 days only
comes out to a total of $280,000, when you

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:02.270
throw in the cost of the rocket launch, training,
and everything else involved in sending tourists

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:07.410
to space, we get a final price tag of $55
million per seat. Three wealthy individuals

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:12.650
have already booked the seats though. This
includes Larry Connor, Mark Pathy, and Eytan

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:18.620
Stibbe. Ironically, despite spending $165
million collectively, these men may not actually

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.870
be the first to rent out the ISS. You may
have heard that Tom Cruise was going to star

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:26.720
in the first movie that’s actually shot
in space. In September of 2020, it was announced

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.360
that Tom Cruise and Director Doug Liman would
be traveling to the ISS in October of 2021

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.670
to start filming. We haven’t received any
updates since then though, and it’s very

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.580
possible that this mission will be pushed
back into 2022. When I said that the Axiom

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:42.840
group wouldn’t be the first to rent out
the ISS though, I wasn’t talking about Tom

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.959
Cruise, I was actually talking about Russia.
You see, when Russia heard about the Tom Cruise

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:52.901
space movie, they decided that they had to
shoot a movie in space as well. Russia plans

00:03:52.901 --> 00:03:57.980
to send their own actress to space in October
of 2021 to film a movie as well. And I have

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:02.830
a feeling that Russia is more dedicated in
actually achieving that timeframe, but we’ll

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:07.040
just have to wait and see. Anyway, getting
back to the axiom trip, though the tourists

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.590
are free to just relax and enjoy their trip,
they revealed that they’re looking to conduct

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:16.160
a plethora of experience and educational activities.
So, I guess it won’t be just leisure after

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.060
all. Aside from sending tourists to the ISS
with the help of SpaceX, Axiom is looking

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.020
to send tourists to their own space station.
At the start of 2020, Axiom received a contract

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:32.520
from NASA worth up to $140 million to build
a commercial module for the ISS. The module

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.940
is officially supposed to be used for research
and other applications. The module is meant

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:41.310
for commercial use though, so maybe it’s
meant for private research teams as well as

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:46.080
tourists interested in conducting their own
research. Aside from this research module,

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.851
Axiom is also looking to add habitation and
tourist modules onto the ISS as well. Adding

00:04:50.851 --> 00:04:55.389
dedicated habitation and tourist modules would
complete the first steps in actually transitioning

00:04:55.389 --> 00:04:59.950
the ISS into a space hotel as opposed to simply
being a research facility that regular people

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:05.490
can visit. The first of these modules is expected
to be launched in the second half of 2024,

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:09.120
and Axiom hopes to continue adding modules
throughout the second half of the decade.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.740
Eventually, as Axiom's additions to the ISS
start to become too big or Nasa decides to

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:18.570
deorbit the ISS, Axiom is looking to disconnect
their modules from the ISS and form their

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:22.700
own commercial space station. This would be
the first space station dedicated to hosting

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.790
private tourists and researchers. Axiom is
not the only company looking to capitalize

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:32.169
on space tourism though, another company with
even grander visions is voyager. Unlike the

00:05:32.169 --> 00:05:36.761
ISS and Axiom’s space station which have
components of research and education, Voyager

00:05:36.761 --> 00:05:41.060
is looking to build a space station completely
dedicated to hospitality and tourism. And

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:45.750
their design is literally straight out of
a sci-fi movie. First of all, Voyager is attempting

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:50.490
to simulate gravity which no other space station
has attempted to do. Just like the movies,

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:53.920
Voyager plans to make a spinning station that
will basically pin tourists to the walls of

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.520
the station. The rotation is expected to simulate
⅙ of earth’s gravity, and all habitation

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.970
and activity modules will have this simulated
gravity. Voyager also understands that one

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:08.620
of the main reasons that people want to visit
space is to experience weightlessness. So,

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:12.919
they have arranged a solution for this as
well. Whenever you’re not sleeping or chilling

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:16.601
in one of their habitation modules, you can
take the elevator to the center of the space

00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:21.760
station which will offer a zero gravity experience.
In terms of safety, Voyager plans to incorporate

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.740
44 emergency return vehicles into their space
station which is basically one for each habitation

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:31.600
module. These emergency return vehicles vaguely
resemble nasa’s space shuttle, and they

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.940
will be capable of completing an automated
flight back to earth. As for amenities, Voyager

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:40.300
is looking to match luxury hotels on earth
as much as possible. Their entry level hotel

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:45.320
suite offers 30 square meters or 320 square
feet of living space. These suites will support

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:49.611
up to 2 people and offer private bathrooms
which include both toilets and showers. If

00:06:49.611 --> 00:06:53.289
you want something bigger, you can spring
for their luxury villas which are absolutely

00:06:53.289 --> 00:06:58.710
massive. Each villa is expected to have 500
square meters or 5300 square feet of living

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:03.520
space. These villas feature cooking facilities,
three bathrooms, and can support up to 16

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:08.780
people each. Voyager is looking to provide
a gym and activity module as well. They claim

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:12.880
that these modules will be 7 meters tall and
support basketball, weight lifting equipment,

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:16.660
and cardio equipment. I don’t think anyone
visiting the space hotel is looking to get

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:21.060
a serious workout in, but it would be quite
interesting to try these tasks in an environment

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:25.440
with ⅙ the gravity of earth. Aside from
a gym, Voyager is also looking to offer a

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:30.470
restaurant and bar. Apparently, the restaurant
will feature iconic astronaut food like freeze

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:34.430
dried ice cream, but they’ll also serve
gourmet food that is delivered to the station

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:39.550
on a biweekly basis. The entire station is
expected to offer a total of 11,600 square

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:45.940
meters or 125,000 square feet of habitable
space. This is enough to support 280 guests

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:50.910
and 112 crew members. Considering the large
capacity of the station, John Blincow, the

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:55.440
leader of the effort, is hoping to bus customers
in using starships. That’s all great and

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:01.410
all, but when will it open and how much will
it cost? Well, their goal is to open in 2027,

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:06.000
so not that far from now. But the ticket price
is of course quite high given the scope of

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.170
the trip. Right now, a 3.5 day stay on the
voyager space station is expected to cost

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:16.820
$5 million per person. And that’s not including
the starship ticket there and back. Now, it

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:21.340
should be noted that visions like this in
the past have not exactly panned out. Orionspan

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:25.540
for instance pitched the idea of building
a small tourist space station in 2018. Their

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:30.650
vision was nowhere near as grand as voyagers,
but unfortunately, they were unable to raise

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:34.789
enough money and they would end up returning
all the money they raised and shutting down

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:38.029
the project. This isn’t to say that Voyager
will fail, but simply that the process of

00:08:38.029 --> 00:08:42.479
building a space hotel is still extremely
difficult. Nonetheless, with Nasa opening

00:08:42.479 --> 00:08:47.220
up the ISS for space tourism, multiple companies
working on space hotels, and rapid progress

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.240
with starship, I think it’s just a matter
of time until we see a full on space hotel

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:56.889
like Voyager envisions. Will we see it in
the 2020s, probably not, but will we see it

00:08:56.889 --> 00:09:02.160
in the 2030s, quite possible. When do you
guys think we’ll see full on space hotels?

